{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift (roughly 2–4 EUR early horizon, widening to ~6–10 EUR by weeks 3–4).",
    "Trend/mean-reversion mismatch: during downtrends the model assumes partial rebound/sideways behavior; reality continues to drift lower. Increase bearish trend persistence (reduce mean-reversion strength) when the last 10–15 days show a clear negative slope.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: forecast error grows with lead time (convex). Use a lead-time scaling correction where the downward adjustment increases with horizon (e.g., small at days 1–5, moderate at days 6–15, large at days 16–30).",
    "Failure to capture drawdown acceleration: after breaks below recent support (new 20–30d lows), realized prices fall faster than forecast. Add an extra downside penalty when spot is making fresh multi-week lows.",
    "Overestimation of rebound after small upticks: single-day/short-run bounces in history lead the model to project a stronger recovery than occurs. Damp positive impulse response (cap rebound magnitude) unless confirmed by multiple consecutive higher closes."
  ]
}
